ve that whole  situation with uh with emil blonsky i  love what they're doing with this  character now one of the cool things  here is that whole scene with emil  blonsky where it shows that he's escaped  and that he's in the cage match you know  when that took place during shang chi  that tells us exactly where the tv show  resides and it happens alongside shang  chi and that's why i say this whole  situation with emil blonsky in the third  act of this episode is incredibly  important because what this establishes  if it hasn't established it already is  that the marvel cinematic universe is  heavily playing the comic book game in  terms of how they release their films  and tv shows it wasn't like that in the  first 10 years of the mcu with the  exception of maybe one or two movies if  that it all followed a linear chronology  it all followed a straightforward  chronology and if things were out of  continuity meaning that the the newest  movie was not directly after or taking  place during the previous movie that  they would tell us in that movie with  the tv shows it's a little murky  sometimes because what they're doing now  is they're releasing the movies and the  tv shows the same way marvel comics  releases their comic books the same way  they publish their comics so the best  way to follow the marvel cinematic  universe right now is to treat it as  though it's a movie version of a marvel  comics publishing initiative right  they're releasing their movies and tv  shows the same way marvel comics  releases their comic books tie-ins and  main events things like that so you can  largely look at all the movies that we  get right everything after the events of  infinity war and avengers inn game  running all the way up to avengers  secret wars and the kane dynasty and all  that kind of stuff you can treat that as  quote unquote the main story arc and  then the tv shows are the tie-in comics  they fill in the gaps in between and let  us know what's taking place betw